          "source_quote": "καὶ τοὺς ἀγαθοὺς εἰς τὸ θεοῦ δεῖπνον εἰσάγειν ἐκ τῆς μεταμελείας",
          "english_quote": "and to bring into God’s banquet those who have become good through a change of heart",
          "issue": "The draft interprets ἐκ τῆς μεταμελείας as explaining how the people became good. That contextual attachment is defensible, but another material reading attaches the phrase to εἰσάγειν: their change of heart or regret supplies the grounds for bringing the good into the banquet. The lexical note discusses meanings of μεταμέλεια but presents its production of goodness as established rather than disclosing this attachment alternative.",
          "suggested_revision": "Expand the note to preserve both relationships: “those who have become good through a change of heart” and “to bring the good into God’s banquet on account of their change of heart.” Explain why the main text selects the former. Alternatively, propose a more attachment-transparent rendering, such as “and to bring the good into God’s banquet as a result of their change of heart,” with a note explaining what the causal phrase may qualify. Any updated wording or notes require fresh review.",
